Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON COURT - DAY

Period live-action London, 1843. Snow and brown fog choke a narrow court beneath a weathered sign: SCROOGE AND MARLEY.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead, to begin with. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail. Scrooge knew it, for he had been Marley's partner, sole friend, and sole mourner.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

EBENEZER SCROOGE, a gaunt elderly man with a pointed nose, wiry grey hair, and severe black suit, guards a meagre coal fire.

Beyond his open door, BOB CRATCHIT, a slight, middle-aged clerk in a threadbare dark suit and long white comforter, copies letters beside one dying coal.

FRED, Scrooge's ruddy, brown-haired young nephew, strides in glowing from the cold.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure.

SCROOGE
What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Every idiot who goes about with “Merry Christmas” on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas as a good time — a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time — when men and women open their shut-up hearts freely. Though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe it has done me good. God bless it!

Bob involuntarily applauds, then extinguishes his last spark while pretending to tend it.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation.

FRED
Come! Dine with us tomorrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you. Why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! And a happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without an angry word. MR. HARTLEY and MR. WEST, portly gentlemen carrying subscription papers, enter.

MR. HARTLEY
At this festive season, it is more than usually desirable that we make some provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. HARTLEY
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR. HARTLEY
They are. Many cannot go there, and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

The gentlemen withdraw. A church bell tolls through the fog.

Bob appears in his hat and white comforter.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day tomorrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

BOB CRATCHIT
Yes, sir. Merry Christmas, sir.

Scrooge answers with a growl. Bob hurries joyfully into the snow.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - COURTYARD - NIGHT

Scrooge crosses the black courtyard and inserts his key into a massive door.

The brass knocker becomes MARLEY'S livid face, spectacles raised upon its ghostly forehead.

Scrooge recoils. The face becomes a knocker again.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He enters and slams the door.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

In his nightcap, dressing gown, and slippers, Scrooge huddles over gruel and a miserably low fire.

A disused bell begins to swing. Soon every bell in the building CLAMOURS.

Silence.

A heavy chain CLANKS below, mounts the stairs, and comes straight through the locked door.

MARLEY'S GHOST appears, transparent in pigtail, waistcoat, tights, and boots. A chain of cashboxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and steel purses winds around him.

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much.

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you, then?

MARLEY
In life, I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you!

Marley gives a dreadful cry and shakes his chain. His bandage slips, and his lower jaw drops upon his breast.

Scrooge falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellow men. If it goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

Marley lifts the terrible chain.

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link and yard by yard. Is its pattern strange to you?

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! Mankind was my business. Charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence were all my business!

Marley leans close, his dead eyes fixed upon Scrooge.

MARLEY
I am here tonight to warn you that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Expect the first when the bell tolls One.

The window flies open. Marley floats into a night crowded with chained phantoms reaching helplessly toward the poor below.

The spirits dissolve into fog. Scrooge shuts the window and tests his undisturbed locks.

SCROOGE
Hum—

He cannot finish the word.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Still dressed, Scrooge falls across his curtained bed.

The church clock races through twelve strokes, pauses, then tolls ONE.

A clear light floods the room. A hand draws Scrooge's curtains aside.

CHRISTMAS PAST stands there, child-sized yet ancient, with long white hair, an unwrinkled face, a white tunic trimmed in summer flowers, and a holly branch. A jet of light burns from its crown.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit whose coming was foretold to me?

CHRISTMAS PAST
I am.

SCROOGE
Who and what are you?

CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past. Rise, and walk with me.

The Spirit touches Scrooge's heart. Bedroom walls vanish into winter daylight.

INT. OLD BOARDING-SCHOOL SCHOOLROOM - DAY

A vast, cold schoolroom stands deserted for Christmas.

BOY SCROOGE, twelve, thin and brown-haired, reads alone beside a feeble fire. Imaginary adventurers shimmer around his desk.

The old Scrooge sits beside his forgotten self and weeps.

CHRISTMAS PAST
A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left here still.

SCROOGE
Poor boy. There was a boy singing a Christmas carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something. That's all.

The door bursts open. FAN, his delicate little sister in a red wool cloak, runs to Boy Scrooge and embraces him.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. We are to be together all the Christmas long!

Boy Scrooge laughs and lets Fan pull him toward the door.

CHRISTMAS PAST
She had a large heart. She died a woman and had one child — your nephew.

Scrooge watches Fan disappear into the snow.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

YOUNG SCROOGE, now a slim apprentice of twenty, works beneath a high desk with another apprentice.

FEZZIWIG, round, rosy, and splendid in a Welsh wig and capacious waistcoat, claps his hands.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, my boys! No more work tonight. Christmas Eve! Clear away, and let's have lots of room here!

Shutters slam. Desks roll aside. Lamps blaze, the fire roars, and the warehouse fills with food, music, workers, and dancing families.

Fezziwig and MRS. FEZZIWIG lead the dance, laughing as Young Scrooge joins in.

CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy, to make our service a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

Scrooge's smile fades.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

Young Scrooge, older and more severe, sits beside BELLE, a fair young woman in a plain mourning dress. Firelight shines through her tears.

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

YOUNG SCROOGE
I am not changed towards you.

BELLE
You are changed. I release you, with a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen.

Belle leaves him alone.

Years sweep across the room. Belle, now a matron, laughs beside her husband and children while Scrooge watches the family warmth he surrendered.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Why do you delight to torture me?

He seizes the Spirit's extinguisher-cap and forces it over the blazing light.

A flood of light escapes beneath it, then snaps to darkness.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes in bed. A ruddy glow streams beneath the adjoining door.

He rises and follows it.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

The room has become a grove of holly, mistletoe, and ivy. A mighty fire illuminates heaps of turkeys, geese, sausages, puddings, fruit, and steaming punch.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT, a joyous giant with dark curls, a holly wreath, and a green robe bordered in white fur, sits upon the feast.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present.

SCROOGE
Conduct me where you will. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge obeys. The room and feast vanish.

INT. CRATCHIT PARLOUR - DAY

A poor four-room home, lovingly dressed for Christmas.

MRS. CRATCHIT, in a twice-turned gown brightened with ribbons, sets the table with PETER, MARTHA, and BELINDA.

Bob enters in his white comforter with TINY TIM upon his shoulder. Tim is a pale, small boy with brown curls, a little crutch, and iron leg brace.

BOB CRATCHIT
He told me coming home that he hoped the people saw him in church because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk and blind men see.

The family gathers around a small goose, mashed potatoes, and a blazing plum pudding. Their mismatched cups are raised.

BOB CRATCHIT
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

Bob takes Tim's withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner and a crutch without an owner. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge bows his head beneath his own words.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die?

Scrooge watches the poor family laugh together, his eyes fixed upon Tiny Tim.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Fred and his dark-haired wife entertain friends around a bright fire and a festive table.

FRED
I am sorry for my uncle. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always. I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not.

Fred raises a glass.

FRED
A merry Christmas and a happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! Uncle Scrooge!

The company drinks. Invisible among them, Scrooge raises an empty hand as though holding a glass.

SCROOGE
Here is a new game, Spirit. One half-hour, only one!

The room vanishes around his pleading smile.

EXT. LONDON SQUARE - NIGHT

Christmas Present now stoops with age. From beneath his green robe emerge two starved, ragged children: IGNORANCE, a wolfish boy, and WANT, a hollow-eyed girl.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes TWELVE. The Spirit and children vanish.

A tall, hooded phantom in a deep black garment glides from the fog. Nothing shows beneath its robe but one pale, outstretched hand.

Scrooge kneels.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future, I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But I know your purpose is to do me good. Lead on, Spirit.

The silent GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME points into darkness.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

Rusty keys, chains, bones, bottles, and greasy rags crowd a low, smoky den.

OLD JOE, a grey-haired fence in a filthy waistcoat, watches MRS. DILBER empty stolen blankets and dark bed-curtains onto the floor.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains! You don't mean to say you took them down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS. DILBER
Yes, I do. Why not? If he had been natural in his lifetime, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death.

Old Joe counts coins into her palm.

MRS. DILBER
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

Her laughter echoes through the shop.

SCROOGE
The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way now.

The Phantom points onward.

INT. DEAD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A bare, uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet lies a plundered corpse, unwatched, unwept, and uncared for.

A cat scratches at the door. Rats gnaw beneath the hearthstone.

The Phantom points toward the covered face.

Scrooge reaches for the sheet, but his hand freezes inches above it.

SCROOGE
I understand you, and I would do it if I could. But I have not the power.

The finger does not move.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit!

The Phantom spreads its robe across the corpse.

INT. CRATCHIT PARLOUR - NIGHT

The room is painfully quiet. Tiny Tim's crutch rests beside an empty stool.

PETER
“And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them.”

Mrs. Cratchit lowers her sewing and wipes her eyes.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known your father walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder very fast indeed. But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so that it was no trouble.

Bob enters slowly, still wearing his long white comforter.

BOB CRATCHIT
I wish you could have gone with me. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. I promised him I would walk there on a Sunday.

His composure breaks.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

The family surrounds him. Scrooge turns desperately to the Phantom.

SCROOGE
Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead.

The Phantom points through the wall.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

Weeds choke a graveyard hemmed in by dark houses.

The Phantom stands beside one neglected stone and points down.

SCROOGE
Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

Snow slides from the stone, revealing the name: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

Scrooge falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge and back again.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! I am not the man I was. Why show me this if I am past all hope?

For the first time, the spectral hand trembles.

Scrooge clutches it with both hands.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. I will not shut out the lessons they teach!

The wind tears at the Phantom's robe.

SCROOGE
Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The Phantom shrinks within Scrooge's grasp until its black robe becomes a bed-curtain and its hand a wooden bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Golden sunlight pours across Scrooge, clinging to his own bedpost.

He throws open the curtains. They are intact.

SCROOGE
The shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled! They will be. I know they will!

He laughs and cries while tangling himself in his clothes.

SCROOGE
I am as light as a feather! I am as happy as an angel! A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world!

Church bells ring. Scrooge throws open the window and spots TOM, a sharp-faced boy in Sunday clothes, below.

SCROOGE
What's today, my fine fellow?

TOM
Today? Why, Christmas Day!

SCROOGE
I haven't missed it! Do you know the poulterer's at the corner? Go and buy the prize turkey — the big one! Bring the man back, and I'll give you half-a-crown!

Tom races away.

Scrooge writes BOB CRATCHIT upon a delivery card, chuckling until tears roll down his face.

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

A poulterer's cart bearing an enormous turkey rattles toward Camden Town.

Scrooge, shaved and dressed in his best black suit, walks among the Christmas crowds with a delighted smile.

He sees Mr. Hartley approaching and takes both his hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. Allow me to ask your pardon.

Scrooge whispers an amount into Mr. Hartley's ear.

MR. HARTLEY
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it. Will you do me that favour?

MR. HARTLEY
My dear sir, I don't know what to say to such munificence—

SCROOGE
Don't say anything. Come and see me. Bless you!

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - DAY

Fred and his wife inspect their Christmas table.

The door opens a crack. Scrooge peers around it.

SCROOGE
Fred! It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in?

Fred stares, then seizes Scrooge's hand and pulls him into a joyous embrace.

Scrooge laughs with the family he once refused.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

The following morning, Bob slips in eighteen minutes late and hurries to his stool.

Scrooge watches from his open door, struggling to conceal a smile.

SCROOGE
What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. It shall not be repeated.

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

Bob shrinks toward the ruler.

Scrooge leaps up and claps him on the back.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob stares at him in alarm.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year. I'll raise your salary and endeavour to assist your struggling family.

Scrooge throws open the coal box.

SCROOGE
Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob's bewilderment gives way to a grateful smile.

INT. CRATCHIT PARLOUR - DAY

The following Christmas, the repaired room glows with a generous fire.

Scrooge serves pudding beside Tiny Tim, alive and laughing. Bob, Mrs. Cratchit, Peter, Martha, and Belinda crowd happily around them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father. It was always said of him that he knew how to keep Christmas well.

Tiny Tim raises his cup toward Scrooge.

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.

THE END
